55, 48.39, 48.413, 48.436, 48.494, 48.541, 48.599, 48.669, 48.703, 48.727, 48.75, 48.796, 48.819, 48.878, 48.924, 48.97, 49.04, 49.087, 49.203, 49.226, 49.284, 49.354, 49.412, 49.446, 49.504, 49.586, 49.644, 49.69, 49.76, 49.783, 49.841, 49.888, 49.98, 50.062, 50.143, 50.224, 50.375, 50.816, 50.898, 50.944, 50.991, 51.037, 51.072, 51.13, 51.165, 51.223, 51.292, 51.35, 51.409, 51.478, 51.571, 51.641, 51.687, 51.734, 51.85, 51.966, 52.082, 52.14, 52.198, 52.244, 52.302, 52.361, 52.419, 52.477, 52.523, 52.593, 52.639, 52.686, 52.86, 52.929, 53.452]}, "text": "So that is the myth. Is there anything solid underneath it? In 1914, the palaeontologist Oh-TAY-nee-oh AH-bel thought there might be. During the Ice Age, dwarf elephants lived on islands across the Mediterranean: Sicily, Malta, Crete, Cyprus, and a scattering of Aegean islands. They descended from a straight-tusked elephant about four metres tall, and island life shrank them. Their bones turn up in caves, and still do. Now look at the skull from the front. The eye sockets are small, and set low at the sides. What dominates is a huge hole in the middle: the opening for the trunk. AH-bel pictured a sailor, sheltering in a cave and lighting a fire, who sees a skull sticking out of the clay, with one great eye. Everything else, he wrote, was added later. It is imagination, not evidence.", "orig": "So that is the myth. Is there anything solid underneath it? In 1914, the palaeontologist Othenio Abel thought there might be. During the Ice Age, dwarf elephants lived on islands across the Mediterranean: Sicily, Malta, Crete, Cyprus, and a scattering of Aegean islands. They descended from a straight-tusked elephant about four metres tall, and island life shrank them. Their bones turn up in caves, and still do. Now look at the skull from the front. The eye sockets are small, and set low at the sides. What dominates is a huge hole in the middle: the opening for the trunk. Abel pictured a sailor, sheltering in a cave and lighting a fire, who sees a skull sticking out of the clay, with one great eye. Everything else, he wrote, was added later. It is imagination, not evidence."}